berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (sensible(x) → perceived_by_sense(x))" and "∀x (perceived_by_sense(x) → idea(x))" and "∀x (idea(x) → ¬exists_unperceived(x))"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has "objects_sensed" and "sensed_ideas" and "¬◇ideas_unperceived"; they differ when only "the fore-mentioned objects (houses, mountains, rivers, all sensible objects) are the things we perceive by sense", "what we perceive by sense is nothing besides our own ideas or sensations" and "possibly, some one of our ideas or sensations, or some combination of them, exists unperceived" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the fore-mentioned objects (houses, mountains, rivers, all sensible objects) are the things we perceive by sense", "what we perceive by sense is nothing besides our own ideas or sensations" and "possibly, some one of our ideas or sensations, or some combination of them, exists unperceived" are true. Is S3, "For, what are the fore-mentioned objects but the things we perceive by sense? and what do we PERCEIVE BESIDES OUR OWN IDEAS OR SENSATIONS? and is it not plainly repugnant that any one of these, or any combination of them, should exist unperceived?", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has "objects_sensed ∧ sensed_ideas → □(objects_unperceived → ideas_unperceived)"; they differ when only "the fore-mentioned objects (houses, mountains, rivers, all sensible objects) are the things we perceive by sense" and "what we perceive by sense is nothing besides our own ideas or sensations" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (sensible(x) → ¬exists_unperceived(x))"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has "¬◇objects_unperceived"; they differ when only "possibly, sensible objects have an existence, natural or real, distinct from their being perceived by the understanding" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "possibly, sensible objects have an existence, natural or real, distinct from their being perceived by the understanding" is true. Is the conclusion, "But, with how great an assurance and acquiescence soever this principle may be entertained in the world, yet whoever shall find in his heart to call it in question may, if I mistake not, perceive it to involve a manifest contradiction.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 takes for granted "if (the fore-mentioned objects (houses, mountains, rivers, all sensible objects) are the things we perceive by sense and what we perceive by sense is nothing besides our own ideas or sensations), then (necessarily, (if sensible objects have an existence, natural or real, distinct from their being perceived by the understanding, then some one of our ideas or sensations, or some combination of them, exists unperceived))"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 do not
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the fore-mentioned objects (houses, mountains, rivers, all sensible objects) are the things we perceive by sense and what we perceive by sense is nothing besides our own ideas or sensations), then (necessarily, (if sensible objects have an existence, natural or real, distinct from their being perceived by the understanding, then some one of our ideas or sensations, or some combination of them, exists unperceived))"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
